WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:02.210
(upbeat orchestral music)
- You thought you knew crêpes,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.890
but you were wrong.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.483
Clickbait-y opening
line, and cue title card.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.830
(gentle string music)

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.540
This whole story requires
you to know one basic fact:

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.725
This is not a crêpe.

00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:20.060
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.460
- [Narrator] It may look, smell,

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.907
and taste like what you
might call a crêpe, but...

00:00:24.907 --> 00:00:27.875
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:30.042
(dinging)

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.520
- [Narrator] This
distinction is a big deal.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.050
And you should listen to Jean Paul

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.890
because Jean Paul is the
current Grand Commando

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.050
of the Brotherhood of the Galette,

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.260
whose headquarters are
here in Pipriac, France,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.460
a city in the northern region of Brittany,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.000
or Breton as they say here.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.850
This group, comprised of 25
members, has one purpose:

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.510
to keep the tradition of
the Breton galette alive,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.330
and to protect the original
recipe and its ingredients.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.020
So let's start at the beginning.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.551
What is a galette?

00:01:01.551 --> 00:01:03.447
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:01:03.447 --> 00:01:07.364
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.781
- [Narrator] So what's the
best way to serve a galette?

00:01:38.781 --> 00:01:42.698
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.800
- [Narrator] Clearly, you're
wondering about the costumes.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.280
Don't worry, we asked.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.197
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:20.209 --> 00:02:21.810
- [Narrator] All of this is to say

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.945
with tradition comes a
sort of responsibility.

00:02:24.945 --> 00:02:28.862
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.330
- [Narrator] Plus, this seems like

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.307
a fun group to be a part of, right?

00:02:43.723 --> 00:02:46.806
(electronic dinging)

